  "narration": {
    "title": "Toronto Crashes the Party, Milwaukee Holds",
    "intro": "The Brewers still sit on top despite a 3-4 week, but the real motion came further down. Toronto jumped eleven spots on a 5-1 week, and Houston climbed seven while still carrying a losing run differential. Order is starting to firm up as the races tighten.",
    "power_lead": "Milwaukee (88-55) keeps the top spot even after a quiet 3-4 week and a minus-8 run differential, with Cooper Pratt's 1.061 OPS the lone bright spot. The eye-catcher is Toronto, up eleven spots on a 5-1 week and a plus-24 run differential, powered by Kazuma Okamoto and a shutout start from Dylan Cease. Houston made noise too, climbing seven behind a 5-2 week even with a minus-37 season mark still to answer for.",
    "offense_lead": "The Cubs lead the team-OPS board at .772, a step ahead of the Dodgers (.756), and the week backs it up \u2014 Alex Bregman's four homers and Michael Conforto's 1.538 stretch kept Chicago's lineup humming. The Rockies sneaking in at fourth (.740) is the small surprise on the list.",
    "staffs_lead": "The Yankees own the best rotation ERA at 3.32, and Cam Schlittler is the reason it keeps shrinking \u2014 a 1.13 ERA over eight innings this week. Their bullpen tops that chart too at 3.06, making New York the rare club leading both pitching boards while Detroit's rotation (3.48) quietly sits second."
  }
